.TH SLEEP 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
sleep \- suspend execution for an interval
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sleep
.I seconds
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sleep
suspends execution for
.I seconds
seconds.
The process is blocked in the kernel until the tick counter reaches
the target, so a
sleeping process takes no share of the processor, and a background
.B sleep
does not slow the job in front of it.
A signal ends it at once: ^C, or
.IR kill (1).
The operand is a whole decimal number from 0 through 9999.
For example:
.PP
.B sleep 5
.PP
waits five seconds before the next command.
A running interrupt tick source is required, even for a zero-second wait.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/SLEEP  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
time(1), sh(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 when the interval has elapsed, 1 if no usable tick source is available, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
Accurate to within a tick (1/60 second) at each end.
The wait is kernel-timed only while the interrupt tick runs; with no
tick source it refuses rather than hang.
